{"id": "DfJaXKkJRSH", "title": "Video by feartalkies", "description": "On her wedding night, a bride told her husband: \"I must go to another man.\" And he let her go. 💍\n\nLast tale's answer: Vikram said none of them was guilty of the man's death. The snake was helpless prey, held in the hawk's claws. The hawk was only carrying its natural food, and the poison fell by chance. The couple gave their rice out of kindness. A killing needs a will to kill, or at least knowing the harm, and nobody there had either.\n\nYes, wrong was done that day: the dying man blamed an innocent woman, and her husband threw her out for it. But a hasty word or a cruel judgement is not murder. Nobody can be called a murderer for what they never chose, and the guilt falls on anyone who blames them without thinking. But the question was who is to be blamed for the murder? None of them.\n\nVikram & Betaal: The Bride's Promise.\n\nThe parts we couldn't fit in the reel:\n\nHer name was Madanasena, a merchant's daughter in the city of Anangapura, and her husband was Samudradatta. Dharmadatta, a young merchant and her brother's friend, saw her playing in the garden. He could not sleep, dreamed of her all night, and came back next morning to find her alone.\n\nShe told him she was promised to another and begged him to leave. When he said he could not live without her, she feared he would use force. So she promised to visit him after her wedding, and swore an oath to it. Then he let her go.\n\nOn the wedding night, she asked her husband to promise not to punish her before she spoke. Then she told him everything, and said she could not break the law of truth she had kept since childhood.\n\nThe thief called her \"the ornament of the world\" and wanted her, not her gold. When she came back, he let her go, pleased with her truthfulness, and walked with her to guard her all the way home. Her husband saw that she had kept her word and her honour, and lived happily with her ever after.\n\n(From Somadeva's Ocean of Story, the Vetala tales.)\n\nSo, who had the bigger heart? The husband who let her walk… or the thief who let her go? 👇\n\n#vikrambetaal #indianhorror #ghoststories #indianfolklore #halloween", "channel": "feartalkies", "uploader_id": "58803293522", "uploader": "Fear Talkies | Indian Horror", "timestamp": 1793424746, "like_count": 4155, "comment_count": 46, "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/151.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8", "Accept-Language": "en-us,en;q=0.5", "Sec-Fetch-Mode": "navigate", "Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1829116825099640a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 67.126, "asr": 44100, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr2-3.fna.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m78/AQP23SNM_AaFBx6q09WQUXwV8_q1RJwkvUPQF0YcAxbJsEB0AaSaJyz0GXezqS77XeAJPT2aZ5TgAJwMYO1UQ_lgZ_24ZpOcjlPvwDk.mp4?_nc_cat=100&_nc_oc=AdquM7riQsjRUeeqWSgCyfGtER3tv82qYOfzdA6uUS7EOa4Kh1SIao8GVFGJPMyk-NKt5OCM8fwBjLcSaVV1jS2R&_nc_sid=9ca052&_nc_ht=instagram.fblr2-3.fna.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=ncIghDIVR14Q7kNvwFOJKrA&%3D%3D&ccb=17-1&_nc_gid=G59eU_Fa6rkLGjeTyry3pg&_nc_ss=70689&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQRbAcPbzdqBk23p_qWev9wM0Xn3kTf9aRArV_WnlcwPjA&oe=6AF40565", "manifest_stream_number": 0, "is_dash_periods": true, "protocol": "https", "audio_ext": "m4a", "video_ext": "none", "vbr": 0, "abr": 67.126, "resolution": "audio only", "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/151.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8", "Accept-Language": "en-us,en;q=0.5", "Sec-Fetch-Mode": "navigate", "Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "format": "dash-1829116825099640a - audio only (DASH audio)"}, {"url": "https: